<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title><%= vuln.name %> - Vuln-World</title>
  <style>
    body { font-family: 'Segoe UI', Tahoma, Geneva, Verdana, sans-serif; margin: 0; padding: 0; background: #f9fafb; color: #374151; line-height: 1.6; }
    header { background: #1f2937; color: #fff; padding: 15px 30px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: space-between; }
    header a { color: #9ca3af; text-decoration: none; font-weight: bold; }
    header a:hover { color: white; }
    .container { max-width: 900px; margin: 40px auto; background: white; padding: 40px; border-radius: 8px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.05); }
    h1 { margin-top: 0; color: #111827; }
    h2 { color: #1f2937; border-bottom: 2px solid #e5e7eb; padding-bottom: 10px; margin-top: 40px; text-transform: uppercase; font-size: 18px; letter-spacing: 0.05em; }
    .badge { display: inline-block; padding: 4px 8px; background: #e5e7eb; border-radius: 12px; font-size: 12px; font-weight: bold; margin-right: 10px; color: #4b5563; }
    .btn { display: inline-block; padding: 12px 24px; background: #2563eb; color: white; text-decoration: none; border-radius: 6px; font-weight: bold; margin: 20px 0; }
    .btn:hover { background: #1d4ed8; }
    pre { background: #1f2937; color: #f8f8f2; padding: 20px; border-radius: 6px; overflow-x: auto; font-family: monospace; font-size: 14px; }
    .vuln-code { border-left: 4px solid #ef4444; }
    .sec-code { border-left: 4px solid #10b981; }
    .diagram { background: #f3f4f6; padding: 20px; font-family: monospace; white-space: pre; border-radius: 6px; border: 1px solid #e5e7eb; overflow-x: auto; font-size: 14px; line-height: 1.5; }
    ul { padding-left: 20px; }
    li { margin-bottom: 8px; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <a href="/vulnerabilities">← Back to Vulnerabilities</a>
  </header>
  <div class="container">
    <h1><%= vuln.name %></h1>
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.category %></span>
      <span class="badge"><%= vuln.difficulty %></span>
    </div>
    
    <a href="/labs/<%= vuln.lab %>?challenge=<%= vuln.id %>" class="btn">Start Lab (<%= vuln.lab %>)</a>
    
    <h2>What Is It?</h2>
    <p>Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI) occurs when a user input is unsafely embedded into a template on the server side, leading to code execution. The application takes the input and evaluates it using a template engine, allowing an attacker to inject template directives and execute arbitrary code on the server.</p>

    <h2>In Simple Words</h2>
    <p>Imagine giving someone a letter with blank spaces to fill in, but instead of just writing their name, they write instructions that the post office reads and executes directly. By providing malicious template code instead of normal data, the server mistakenly runs the code.</p>

    <h2>Where Does It Appear?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Web pages that generate dynamic content based on user input (e.g., customized emails, PDF generation, or greeting pages).</li>
      <li>Applications using engines like Jinja2, Twig, EJS, Pug, or FreeMarker.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How Does It Occur?</h2>
    <div class="diagram">
User Input ("{{7*7}}") 
   |
   V
App Concatenates Input into Template String:
"Hello, " + "{{7*7}}" + "!"
   |
   V
Template Engine Evaluates:
"Hello, 49!"
    </div>

    <h2>HTTP Example</h2>
    <pre>
POST /render HTTP/1.1
Host: example.com
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

name={{7*7}}
    </pre>

    <h2>Burp Suite Investigation</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Use Burp Suite to intercept a request where you submit user input that is reflected on the page.</li>
      <li>Modify the input parameter in Repeater to include basic template math, such as <code>{{7*7}}</code>, <code>${7*7}</code>, or <code>&lt;%= 7*7 %&gt;</code>.</li>
      <li>Send the request and observe the response. If the application returns <code>49</code>, it is vulnerable to SSTI.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>How Developers Accidentally Create It</h2>
    <p>Vulnerable Code:</p>
    <pre class="vuln-code">
const ejs = require('ejs');
const template = "Hello " + req.query.name + "!";
const rendered = ejs.render(template);
    </pre>
    <p>Secure Code:</p>
    <pre class="sec-code">
const ejs = require('ejs');
const template = "Hello &lt;%= name %&gt;!";
const rendered = ejs.render(template, { name: req.query.name });
    </pre>

    <h2>Impact</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Remote Code Execution (RCE) on the server.</li>
      <li>Full compromise of the application and underlying infrastructure.</li>
      <li>Data exfiltration, including sensitive environment variables and source code.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How to Find It in Real Applications</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Test input fields and URL parameters that reflect data directly.</li>
      <li>Try polyglot template injection payloads.</li>
      <li>Identify the template engine by analyzing error messages or testing different syntax.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Remediation</h2>
    <p>Never concatenate user input directly into template strings. Instead, pass user input as data/context variables to the template engine. Alternatively, use logic-less template engines like Mustache if complex logic is not required.</p>

    <h2>Secure Design Checklist</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Use safe template mechanisms (pass variables as context, not as string concatenation).</li>
      <li>Sanitize and validate input where applicable.</li>
      <li>Run the application in a sandboxed or low-privilege environment.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Lab Investigation Guide (Advanced)</h2>
    <p>In this lab, you are interacting with TemplateWorks, an email campaign management platform. A common architectural pattern for such applications involves a frontend interface to draft campaigns, and a backend rendering service that processes the email templates before sending them. Your goal is to map this architecture, understand how campaigns are built, saved, and rendered, and determine if the rendering pipeline trusts user-supplied template code.</p>
    <p>Think about where the application interprets content versus where it simply stores it as data. Map out all endpoints involved in the creation and preview lifecycle. Can you inject code into the template context?</p>

    <h2>Post-Lab Explanation</h2>
    <p>In TemplateWorks, creating and updating a campaign simply stored your data as strings. However, when you requested a "Preview Notification," the frontend made an internal API call to a rendering service. The rendering service fetched your campaign body and unsafely evaluated the entire body as an EJS template string.</p>
    <p>Because EJS executes the template within a specific server-side context, you were able to break out of simple text rendering and access other variables exposed to that context, such as the internal configuration secret, by injecting <code>&lt;%= config.internalSecret %&gt;</code> into the campaign body.</p>
  </div>
</body>
</html>
